WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:04.667
-Hey, everybody,
welcome to Corrections.

00:00:04.667 --> 00:00:08.200
I was told if I really
want this to be

00:00:08.200 --> 00:00:10.234
in serious consideration
for an Emmy,

00:00:10.234 --> 00:00:12.367
I should be cheerier,

00:00:12.367 --> 00:00:14.968
that it's maybe too surly.

00:00:14.968 --> 00:00:17.300
So I'm gonna make
that effort today.

00:00:17.300 --> 00:00:19.734
I said it's "mash-inations."

00:00:19.734 --> 00:00:21.968
It's actually pronounced
"mack-inations."

00:00:21.968 --> 00:00:23.567
So thank you!

00:00:23.567 --> 00:00:26.701
[ Laughter ]

00:00:34.334 --> 00:00:36.300
We referenced
an M.C. Escher painting.

00:00:36.300 --> 00:00:39.234
We were told he did lithographs,
not paintings,

00:00:39.234 --> 00:00:42.567
but he did do one painting
that he made look

00:00:42.567 --> 00:00:45.801
exactly like a lithograph.

00:00:45.801 --> 00:00:48.400
Classic Escher!

00:00:48.400 --> 00:00:49.801
[ Light laughter ]

00:00:52.634 --> 00:00:54.434
We made a joke in the monologue

00:00:54.434 --> 00:00:56.501
before the first round
of the NFL draft

00:00:56.501 --> 00:00:59.968
congratulating the 31 players

00:00:59.968 --> 00:01:03.167
who were not selected
by the New York Jets.

00:01:03.167 --> 00:01:07.534
Then it turned out the Jets
had three first-round picks,

00:01:07.534 --> 00:01:10.534
so there were only 29 survivors.

00:01:13.067 --> 00:01:17.167
The Jets draft was lauded
by many

00:01:17.167 --> 00:01:18.701
as being an excellent draft.

00:01:18.701 --> 00:01:20.634
And some people said,

00:01:20.634 --> 00:01:25.200
"Hey, you better be careful.
The Jets are good now."

00:01:25.200 --> 00:01:27.767
And it is true.
They always say

00:01:27.767 --> 00:01:32.868
you never want to see the Jets
on your schedule in May.

00:01:32.868 --> 00:01:35.901
[ Laughter ]

00:01:35.901 --> 00:01:39.367
I said that Marshalls
has changing rooms.

00:01:39.367 --> 00:01:40.634
They're fitting rooms.

00:01:40.634 --> 00:01:45.467
I can see why those of you
who engage with this

00:01:45.467 --> 00:01:49.868
would hate hearing me say
"changing rooms,"

00:01:49.868 --> 00:01:54.100
as Jackals are
incapable of change.

00:01:54.100 --> 00:01:57.100
[ Laughter ]

00:01:58.567 --> 00:02:02.434
All right, Rudy Giuliani --
it was Monday's Closer Look.

00:02:02.434 --> 00:02:03.767
We were real happy
when we saw it.

00:02:03.767 --> 00:02:05.767
I'm not gonna lie.
Sometimes things happen.

00:02:05.767 --> 00:02:07.000
Sometimes things that happen
in the news

00:02:07.000 --> 00:02:09.167
makes you real sad,
and you don't even want to

00:02:09.167 --> 00:02:10.467
go to work and talk about it.

00:02:10.467 --> 00:02:12.834
Other times, Rudy Giuliani
makes a video of himself

00:02:12.834 --> 00:02:16.033
on a golf course talking about
how he's got new Cameo --

00:02:16.033 --> 00:02:19.133
you know, get a message
from him on Cameo --

00:02:19.133 --> 00:02:21.901
swings a golf club
back and forth like a weirdo.

00:02:21.901 --> 00:02:24.901
Pretty happy to come into work.

00:02:24.901 --> 00:02:26.667
We showed this.

00:02:26.667 --> 00:02:29.634
We said he looked like
a metronome.

00:02:29.634 --> 00:02:32.033
And I like to give credit where
credit's due.

00:02:32.033 --> 00:02:35.033
One of you said,
"That's a picture of a metronome

00:02:35.033 --> 00:02:39.234
and a New York metro gnome."

00:02:39.234 --> 00:02:42.467
[ Laughter ]

00:02:42.467 --> 00:02:44.567
Very well done.

00:02:44.567 --> 00:02:46.067
All I can say
to whoever wrote that is,

00:02:46.067 --> 00:02:49.167
stop hanging out
with those Jackals.

00:02:49.167 --> 00:02:52.033
You're better than them.

00:02:52.033 --> 00:02:55.501
Seeing a joke like that
in the cesspool --

00:02:55.501 --> 00:02:58.300
it's like -- it's like
watching Matt Damon

00:02:58.300 --> 00:03:01.734
solve the math problem
in "Good Will Hunting."

00:03:03.234 --> 00:03:04.868
[ Light laughter ]

00:03:08.467 --> 00:03:10.534
We made a joke about
how airplane toilets

00:03:10.534 --> 00:03:15.067
are so powerful you could
flush a toaster down one.

00:03:15.067 --> 00:03:18.534
And someone who works
on an airline flight crew said,

00:03:18.534 --> 00:03:19.567
"That's not true.

00:03:19.567 --> 00:03:23.367
Please don't tell people
to do that."

00:03:23.367 --> 00:03:27.100
And so on behalf of hardworking
flight crews everywhere,

00:03:27.100 --> 00:03:28.634
don't do that.

00:03:28.634 --> 00:03:32.434
And if you do,
don't say you heard it from us.

00:03:32.434 --> 00:03:34.868
[ Laughter ]

00:03:36.767 --> 00:03:40.033
We -- oh, by the way, tap water.

00:03:43.300 --> 00:03:45.968
Mmm, that's good, I think.

00:03:45.968 --> 00:03:50.067
We showed a letter from Eminem.

00:03:50.067 --> 00:03:54.601
You all said he misspelled
"found" -- "founf."

00:03:54.601 --> 00:03:55.801
So I reached out to him.

00:03:55.801 --> 00:03:58.167
I followed up with --
'cause he wrote it.

00:04:00.767 --> 00:04:02.234
And they said,
"The jackals are upset

00:04:02.234 --> 00:04:04.300
you put two 'F's in 'found,'"

00:04:04.300 --> 00:04:05.534
and he said,
"It wasn't a mistake.

00:04:05.534 --> 00:04:07.133
The second 'F' is a reminder

00:04:07.133 --> 00:04:08.501
that they can go
[bleep] themselves."

00:04:08.501 --> 00:04:10.033
His words.

00:04:10.033 --> 00:04:12.133
[ Laughter ]

00:04:20.234 --> 00:04:23.067
Uh...

00:04:23.067 --> 00:04:24.667
Samberg --

00:04:24.667 --> 00:04:27.067
Every day we've been
talking about Andy Samberg.

00:04:27.067 --> 00:04:29.701
We haven't been talking
about him every day.

00:04:29.701 --> 00:04:31.367
So back off.

00:04:31.367 --> 00:04:32.734
He does the spelling bee,

00:04:32.734 --> 00:04:34.167
"The New York Times"
spelling bee, every day,

00:04:34.167 --> 00:04:36.033
and he gets Queen Bee,
which is all the words.

00:04:36.033 --> 00:04:38.767
One of you said,
"No, it's not all the words.

00:04:38.767 --> 00:04:41.334
There's just a cutoff point,
and once you get that,

00:04:41.334 --> 00:04:44.100
you're Queen Bee, and you can
keep going if you want."

00:04:44.100 --> 00:04:47.100
You've confused
Queen Bee with Genius.

00:04:47.100 --> 00:04:51.133
Genius means, yes, you can stop,
but there are more to go."

00:04:51.133 --> 00:04:54.901
Andy gets Queen Bee.
He gets all the words.

00:04:54.901 --> 00:04:57.868
And I think the fact
that he gets that every day

00:04:57.868 --> 00:05:01.734
is the clearest possible
reminder to anyone

00:05:01.734 --> 00:05:03.367
that they're not filming
new episodes

00:05:03.367 --> 00:05:04.834
of "Brooklyn Nine-Nine."

00:05:04.834 --> 00:05:06.634
[ Light laughter ]

00:05:09.367 --> 00:05:12.734
Time for our weekly
disappointment.

00:05:12.734 --> 00:05:14.868
I mean, I have no faith, but...

00:05:14.868 --> 00:05:17.200
[ Line ringing ]

00:05:22.234 --> 00:05:24.400
It builds tension.

00:05:24.400 --> 00:05:26.334
It's like "Better Call Saul."

00:05:26.334 --> 00:05:29.267
[ Laughter ]

00:05:29.267 --> 00:05:32.067
[ Ringing continues ]

00:05:35.267 --> 00:05:38.968
I just wish he'd set his phone

00:05:38.968 --> 00:05:42.601
to, like, not answer faster.

00:05:44.434 --> 00:05:47.767
All right.

00:05:47.767 --> 00:05:48.801
FaceTime unavailable.

00:05:48.801 --> 00:05:51.200
Well, thanks for
racing to tell me.

00:05:53.300 --> 00:05:55.767
Let's see what else.
You know what?

00:05:59.667 --> 00:06:01.400
I got other friends.

00:06:01.400 --> 00:06:04.534
[ Laughter ]

00:06:04.534 --> 00:06:06.734
[ Line ringing ]

00:06:15.067 --> 00:06:16.667
Could be calling anyone.

00:06:19.033 --> 00:06:21.367
Be a real bummer
if two people didn't answer.

00:06:24.133 --> 00:06:27.734
Looks like that's how
it's gonna go, though.

00:06:27.734 --> 00:06:32.033
Because if I preset the calls,
the magic's over.

00:06:34.100 --> 00:06:35.868
All right.

00:06:38.067 --> 00:06:41.567
Where were we? Oh, we had a --
[ Cellphone dings ]

00:06:41.567 --> 00:06:44.601
Okay, here's Andy.

00:06:44.601 --> 00:06:47.367
"I'm naked.
Just got out of the shower.

00:06:47.367 --> 00:06:49.234
Tell them I got Queen Bee."

00:06:49.234 --> 00:06:51.400
[ Laughter ]

00:06:56.067 --> 00:06:58.067
We had a joke that
a dog's personality

00:06:58.067 --> 00:07:00.167
had nothing to do
with its breed.

00:07:00.167 --> 00:07:01.734
That was the setup.
There was a new study

00:07:01.734 --> 00:07:04.934
that a dog's personality had
nothing to do with its breed.

00:07:04.934 --> 00:07:07.367
And then our punchline
in the monologue was,

00:07:07.367 --> 00:07:11.901
"For example, a different collie
pushed Timmy into the well."

00:07:11.901 --> 00:07:16.534
Well, there were
some "Lassie" fans

00:07:16.534 --> 00:07:18.968
who know the canon.

00:07:18.968 --> 00:07:24.334
And I was told at no point did
Timmy ever fall into the well...

00:07:26.133 --> 00:07:27.434
...in "Lassie."

00:07:27.434 --> 00:07:30.601
In 1955, there was an episode
called "The Well,"

00:07:30.601 --> 00:07:34.667
and if there was any episode
for Timmy to take the plunge,

00:07:34.667 --> 00:07:37.701
you'd expect to be that one.

00:07:37.701 --> 00:07:41.367
But according to
the IMDB episode guide,

00:07:41.367 --> 00:07:43.868
"An unscrupulous
water company executive

00:07:43.868 --> 00:07:46.901
trying to acquire by any means
water rights from the Miller

00:07:46.901 --> 00:07:51.501
and other farms is pushed
into an old well by Lassie

00:07:51.501 --> 00:07:54.667
when he mistreats Jeff."

00:07:54.667 --> 00:07:57.234
There you go. Our joke
was closer to the truth.

00:07:57.234 --> 00:08:02.701
Jeff was the previous owner,
before Timmy.

00:08:02.701 --> 00:08:04.267
Then there was
a two-part episode

00:08:04.267 --> 00:08:07.834
that aired in 1971 titled
"For the Love of Lassie."

00:08:07.834 --> 00:08:09.968
The well returned,
and according to IMDB,

00:08:09.968 --> 00:08:11.634
"Lassie returns
to the Braddock ranch

00:08:11.634 --> 00:08:13.534
where she left one of her pups
while playing.

00:08:13.534 --> 00:08:16.801
Lassie falls down
an abandoned well.

00:08:16.801 --> 00:08:19.467
The Braddocks begin to dig
to get the heroic collie out

00:08:19.467 --> 00:08:20.868
but are needing help.

00:08:20.868 --> 00:08:23.534
Part two --
according to IMDB again --

00:08:23.534 --> 00:08:25.267
"While the Braddocks work
diligently

00:08:25.267 --> 00:08:27.701
with concerned town folks
to rescue their beloved dog,

00:08:27.701 --> 00:08:30.234
Lassie, still at the bottom
of the well,

00:08:30.234 --> 00:08:33.300
comes face to face
with the corpse

00:08:33.300 --> 00:08:37.167
of an unscrupulous
water company executive

00:08:37.167 --> 00:08:40.734
who, though long dead,
manages to utter the words,

00:08:40.734 --> 00:08:42.601
'Remember me?'"

00:08:42.601 --> 00:08:44.834
[ Light laughter ]

00:08:47.367 --> 00:08:52.300
I would say it would be
traumatic for Lassie

00:08:52.300 --> 00:08:55.434
to fall into a well
16 years after pushing someone

00:08:55.434 --> 00:08:58.267
into the same well.

00:08:58.267 --> 00:08:59.968
But here's the good news --

00:08:59.968 --> 00:09:02.133
not the same Lassie.

00:09:02.133 --> 00:09:05.367
[ Laughter ]

00:09:05.367 --> 00:09:07.167
You guys know how dogs work,
right?

00:09:09.467 --> 00:09:11.367
Hey! [ Chuckles ]

00:09:11.367 --> 00:09:12.801
[ Laughter ]

00:09:15.067 --> 00:09:19.234
And in my digging --
my research, as it were --

00:09:19.234 --> 00:09:21.434
I think I can call it research.

00:09:21.434 --> 00:09:23.234
I feel like I put
as much research

00:09:23.234 --> 00:09:26.100
into each episode
of Corrections

00:09:26.100 --> 00:09:27.968
as anyone who comes
to the conclusion

00:09:27.968 --> 00:09:31.133
that ivermectin's the cure.

00:09:31.133 --> 00:09:33.868
Not nothing.

00:09:33.868 --> 00:09:36.634
You know, but I didn't go,
like, finish vet school.

00:09:40.334 --> 00:09:42.234
Oh, yeah. So, Lassie --

00:09:42.234 --> 00:09:44.667
here's -- this just on
the trivia page of IMDB,

00:09:44.667 --> 00:09:47.434
where, you know, I stumbled
looking for my well answer.

00:09:54.801 --> 00:09:57.267
[ Laughter ]

00:09:58.601 --> 00:10:00.033
Tap water.

00:10:00.033 --> 00:10:02.167
[ Laughter ]

00:10:07.434 --> 00:10:09.834
All the Lassies -- Lassie was
a girl dog in the show.

00:10:09.834 --> 00:10:11.968
All the Lassies were male dogs

00:10:11.968 --> 00:10:15.534
because female collies
tend to blow coat.

00:10:18.167 --> 00:10:20.334
We made a joke about Halloween.

00:10:20.334 --> 00:10:24.267
I know I should explain
what "blowing coat" means.

00:10:24.267 --> 00:10:25.767
And I looked it up,
and I know the answer,

00:10:25.767 --> 00:10:30.033
but I just -- the moment between
when I heard the term

00:10:30.033 --> 00:10:32.501
and found out what it was,

00:10:32.501 --> 00:10:34.534
that was the best part
of my day.

00:10:34.534 --> 00:10:36.501
[ Light laughter ]

00:10:41.701 --> 00:10:44.100
It's just more fun to --
this moment where you're like,

00:10:44.100 --> 00:10:45.801
"Blow coat?"

00:10:48.868 --> 00:10:50.834
[ Light laughter ]

00:10:50.834 --> 00:10:53.601
It sounds like something
a studio executive in 1955

00:10:53.601 --> 00:10:57.033
would say for why they don't
work with female collies.

00:10:57.033 --> 00:10:58.834
[ Laughs ]

00:11:00.801 --> 00:11:03.067
We -- oh, yeah,
we talked about Halloween

00:11:03.067 --> 00:11:06.000
and that if a party had
no sugar, you'd have raisins.

00:11:06.000 --> 00:11:08.801
Raisin -- according to all you,
raisins are high in sugar.

00:11:08.801 --> 00:11:12.901
So best of luck telling that
to a kid who wants Skittles.

00:11:12.901 --> 00:11:14.968
Oh. This is exciting.

00:11:14.968 --> 00:11:19.100
I have an update on my book
in regards to its place

00:11:19.100 --> 00:11:21.434
on "The New York Times"
Best Seller list.

00:11:21.434 --> 00:11:23.000
And the update is

00:11:23.000 --> 00:11:26.634
lists are dumb.
[ Laughter ]

00:11:26.634 --> 00:11:27.734
Like, what are we doing?

00:11:27.734 --> 00:11:29.300
"Who's number one?
Who's number two?"

00:11:29.300 --> 00:11:32.467
These are books.

00:11:32.467 --> 00:11:34.033
They're books.
They're for ki--

00:11:34.033 --> 00:11:35.667
like, to rank them...

00:11:38.100 --> 00:11:40.167
It's not commerce. It's art.

00:11:40.167 --> 00:11:41.801
And they're supposed to
last forever.

00:11:41.801 --> 00:11:44.634
And I just think this very --
I don't know --

00:11:44.634 --> 00:11:46.634
the whole exercise of,
"Who did the best?"

00:11:46.634 --> 00:11:49.200
it's just dumb,

00:11:49.200 --> 00:11:52.601
which is why last week

00:11:52.601 --> 00:11:54.734
I withdrew my book...

00:11:57.000 --> 00:11:58.801
...from consideration.

00:12:03.767 --> 00:12:06.367
Oh. Uh...

00:12:07.534 --> 00:12:10.634
Still updating.

00:12:10.634 --> 00:12:12.801
We don't have
our Jackal mug yet.

00:12:12.801 --> 00:12:15.734
We mentioned that
the merchandise -- NBC --

00:12:15.734 --> 00:12:18.734
it's a little slow
getting new merchandise.

00:12:18.734 --> 00:12:21.200
And just to give you a sense,

00:12:21.200 --> 00:12:23.968
first of all,
of how slow it's moving --

00:12:23.968 --> 00:12:27.033
this was pointed out to me,
so I did not know this --

00:12:27.033 --> 00:12:31.434
one of the things you can get
if you go to the NBC merch store

00:12:31.434 --> 00:12:33.567
and you go to the home page

00:12:33.567 --> 00:12:36.334
and then click "Other Shows"...

00:12:36.334 --> 00:12:38.501
[ Laughter ]

00:12:42.300 --> 00:12:44.200
There's a bunch --
it's not even --

00:12:44.200 --> 00:12:46.000
there's a bunch
on the home page,

00:12:46.000 --> 00:12:49.968
and then those little arrows
so you can keep...

00:12:49.968 --> 00:12:51.834
nope, nope, nope.

00:12:53.501 --> 00:12:54.801
[ Light laughter ]

00:12:58.267 --> 00:13:01.167
So there's -- this is here,
which is that --

00:13:01.167 --> 00:13:03.367
that's a mug you can buy now.

00:13:03.367 --> 00:13:05.000
And here's -- I think
this gives you a sense

00:13:05.000 --> 00:13:06.968
of, like, the speed it moves.

00:13:06.968 --> 00:13:10.601
This is what -- this is
an actual description.

00:13:10.601 --> 00:13:12.334
"Stay up late
and stay caffeinated

00:13:12.334 --> 00:13:14.434
while showing you're a big fan
of Seth.

00:13:14.434 --> 00:13:16.167
The 'Late Night
with Seth Meyers' logo mug

00:13:16.167 --> 00:13:20.067
features the new
'Late Night' logo

00:13:20.067 --> 00:13:22.801
on the 'As Seen on
Seth Meyers Show' mug.

00:13:22.801 --> 00:13:25.501
With this unique mug in hand,
you are ready to cheer on

00:13:25.501 --> 00:13:30.067
Seth Meyers in his new
position."

00:13:30.067 --> 00:13:33.534
[ Laughter ]

00:13:33.534 --> 00:13:36.767
"His new position."

00:13:36.767 --> 00:13:38.434
Year eight.

00:13:38.434 --> 00:13:41.000
[ Laughter ]

00:13:42.934 --> 00:13:46.834
If you walked up to me
with one of these mugs

00:13:46.834 --> 00:13:50.567
and said, "Cheers...
on your new pos-- "

00:13:50.567 --> 00:13:54.100
I wouldn't know what the [bleep]
you were talking about.

00:13:54.100 --> 00:13:56.934
[ Laughter ]

00:14:01.601 --> 00:14:04.033
We also have a notebook,
and I didn't -- this is --

00:14:04.033 --> 00:14:06.534
also, somebody shamed it
and said, why --

00:14:06.534 --> 00:14:09.167
like, a bunch of these
are [chuckles] exclusive.

00:14:09.167 --> 00:14:11.801
And they said,
"What does exclusive mean?

00:14:11.801 --> 00:14:13.634
That I got the Internet
and a credit card

00:14:13.634 --> 00:14:15.534
and I did some digging?"
And yep.

00:14:17.734 --> 00:14:22.167
So we have a notebook
and a hat and pencils.

00:14:22.167 --> 00:14:24.467
And this is one of the photos.

00:14:27.467 --> 00:14:31.100
And someone said, "Why is Amber
so happy in this picture?"

00:14:31.100 --> 00:14:34.534
She just heard a Jackal
fell down a well.

00:14:34.534 --> 00:14:36.467
[ Laughter ]

00:14:39.367 --> 00:14:41.667
She just did a line of "coat."

00:14:41.667 --> 00:14:43.767
[ Laughter ]

00:14:47.167 --> 00:14:49.634
I think overall...

00:14:49.634 --> 00:14:53.067
I think that was very cheerful.

00:14:53.067 --> 00:14:55.534
I don't think it was as long --
How long was it?

00:14:55.534 --> 00:14:56.834
-Almost 15.
Okay.

00:14:56.834 --> 00:14:58.334
So I think that's --
you know, that's decent.

00:14:58.334 --> 00:15:00.501
And I think we're providing
a nice cushion.

00:15:00.501 --> 00:15:02.234
You know, I think there was
a risk there for a while

00:15:02.234 --> 00:15:04.567
that, you know, we were gonna

00:15:04.567 --> 00:15:06.601
qualify as an actual sitcom.

00:15:06.601 --> 00:15:09.167
[ Laughs ]

00:15:09.167 --> 00:15:11.467
[ Singsong voice ]
Well, I'm just so very happy

00:15:11.467 --> 00:15:14.133
that my friends don't answer
their [bleep] phones!

